{"id":"235090d6-506c-4b51-8f36-0c7b519a46af","object":"chat.completion","created":1793333293,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"Read environments are registered; now applying the first edits (env factory method + stats `extra`).\n\n```python\nr = replace_text(\n    \"deepinfer/environ.py\",\n    \"\"\"    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none() -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        return EnvVar(lambda v: int(v) if v is not None else None)\n\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none() -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        return EnvVar(lambda v: int(v) if v is not None else None)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(*, minimum: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Optional int; unset keeps None, otherwise must parse and be >= minimum.\\\"\\\"\\\"\n\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{v!r}, must be an integer >= {minimum}\")\n            return value\n\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n\"\"\",\n    env_hash,\n)\nprint(r[\"rc\"] if \"rc\" in r else r)\n```","reasoning_content":"Let me start editing. I need to make three edits:\n\n1. environ.py: add `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none(...)` with validation >= 32, empty string error, variable name in error.\n\nNote `int_or_none` uses `int(v) if v is not None else None`. Empty string → `int(\"\")` raises ValueError with message `invalid literal for int() with base 10: ''`. The EnvVar `__get__` wraps with `f\"{self.name}: {e}\"`, so name included. But we need >=32 check. Add a factory method e.g. `EnvVar.int_or_none_min(min_value=32)`.\n\nActually the spec: \"描述符类可以新增辅助工厂方法（用于创建配置项的方法）\". Let's add:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(*, minimum: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{v!r} must be >= {minimum}\")\n            return value\n\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nEmpty string → int(\"\") raises ValueError → wrapped with name. Good.\n\nThen in envs Logging section:\n```python\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(minimum=32)\n```\n\n2. logger.py: extend NewLineFormatter with keyword-only `max_message_chars` validation, truncation applied to message body after args substitution. But `super().format(record)` builds the whole string including prefix, exception, stack_info. Requirement: truncate only message body, not prefix/exception/stack_info.\n\nApproach: compute message via `record.getMessage()` (after args substitution) → `message`. Then truncate → new message. Then format using a copy? But we must not modify the original record. Requirement 5 says can't rewrite shared original record's msg/args/exc_info/stack_info. So create a shallow copy of record with message replaced.\n\nTrick: `logging.Formatter.format` uses `record.message` set by `formatMessage` (via `record.getMessage()`). Actually `Formatter.format`:\n```python\nrecord.message = record.getMessage()\nif self.usesTime(): record.asctime = ...\ns = self.formatMessage(record)\nif record.exc_info: ...\nif record.stack_info: ...\n```\nSo we can copy the record, set copy.msg = truncated message, copy.args = None (or ()), and call super().format(copy). But careful: cached `record.message`... `getMessage` uses `msg % args` if args. If args is empty tuple, `if self.args:` false → returns str(msg). So set msg=truncated, args=None.\n\nBut exc_info/stack_info are kept in the copy so they format fully. Also existing code uses `record.message` in format() for the multi-line prefix computation — with the copy approach, `record.message` is not set on the original. Careful: existing code:\n\n```python\nmsg = super().format(record)\nif \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:\n    prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]\n```\n\nWe must preserve behavior. Approach: use a helper:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    record = self._prepare_record(record)  # returns record or copy\n    msg = super().format(record)\n    ...\n```\n\nBut `record.message` after super().format(copy) is set on the copy, which we then use in the multiline prefix logic — fine since we reference the local `record` variable.\n\nCareful about the requirement that the trailing/prefix logic be unchanged. Let's write:\n\n```python\n    def _truncate_message(self, message: str) -> str:\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        if budget is None or len(message) <= budget:\n            return message\n        marker = _OMITTED_MARKER\n        remaining = budget - len(marker)\n        if remaining <= 0:\n            return marker[:budget]\n        head_len = (remaining + 1) // 2\n        tail_len = remaining - head_len\n        return message[:head_len] + marker + (message[-tail_len:] if tail_len else \"\")\n```\n\nWait: \"开头保留向上取整的一半，末尾保留其余部分\" with budget 32, marker 15 → remaining 17, head 9, tail 8. (17+1)//2 = 9. ✓. And `message[-tail_len:]` when tail_len==0 → `message[0:]` wrong, guard needed. Actually tail_len=0 only when remaining==1 → head=1, tail=0. Guard with `if tail_len`.\n\nformat:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        rec = record\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(rec, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = rec.getMessage()\n            truncated = self._truncate_message(message)\n            if truncated is not message:\n                rec = logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)  # copy\n                rec.msg = truncated\n                rec.args = None\n                rec.message = truncated\n        msg = super().format(rec)\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and rec.message:\n            prefix = msg[: msg.find(rec.message)]\n            msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, `logging.makeLogRecord(dict)` creates new LogRecord and updates __dict__, so exc_info etc. carried. But it also re-runs `__init__`? makeLogRecord: `rv = LogRecord(None, None, \"\", 0, \"\", (), None, None); rv.__dict__.update(dict)`. Fine. But `rec.exc_text = None` — original's exc_text may already be set (if a previous handler formatted it). That's fine; exc_text carried over via __dict__ update.\n\nAlternatively use `copy.copy(record)` - but copy module import; `logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)` is simpler but note it copies dict, and updates rv.__dict__ — that's a shallow copy effectively. Good.\n\nActually simpler: `rec = logging.LogRecord(...)`. Use makeLogRecord.\n\nCareful: `record.message` in super().format sets rec.message = rec.getMessage() which is msg % args with args None → str(msg) = truncated. Good.\n\nAlso the color and formatTime preserved since same class.\n\nDo we need `self.max_message_chars` validation: keyword-only, int >= 32, bool not accepted, None ok:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None):\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be an int >= 32 or None, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n            if max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\n\nAlso maybe accept objects that are int subclasses. Fine.\n\nDefault logger init: `_setup_logger()` should pass `max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`. Import inside function to avoid circular import? environ.py imports os, threading only — logger imports environ — check environ doesn't import logger. It doesn't. But safe: import at module top `from deepinfer.environ import envs`. Let's check logger.py top imports; adding module-level import is fine. Hmm, deepinfer/environ.py has no imports of logger. I'll do a module-level import.\n\nWait, careful: the integration test \"实际加载工作副本的配置、日志模块\" — importing logger triggers `_setup_logger()` at import, which accesses envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS → reads env var at import time, caching. That could be an issue: if test sets env var then reloads the module... They test \"重新执行默认日志初始化时，将当时的配置值应用到新的格式化器\". Fine either way. But at import time reading env var caches None. If the test sets env var after import and then calls... hmm. Risk: reading config at import time caches None, so later a test setting env var and calling init_logger/_setup_logger would still get None unless refreshed. Spec says cache is at first access. So that's consistent behavior; the test presumably uses rearm. But there's risk that test does: set env, rearm, `_setup_logger()`, check truncation. Fine.\n\nAlternative to reduce risk: import inside `_setup_logger()` lazily (still caches on first setup). Same thing. I'll do module-level import.\n\nHmm, but caching at import time means: test sets env var DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS=64 before importing deepinfer.logger → import reads 64. Good. If test sets after import and calls _setup_logger without rearm → cached None → no truncation → could fail if test expects 64. But spec explicitly says cached behavior with rearm needed. The tests were designed against a reference implementation; likely they set env before import or rearm.\n\nActually wait — maybe safer to make `_setup_logger` read env var... it must use the centralized config with cache. Follow the spec.\n\n3. stats_logger.py `_log_worker`: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nNow also confirm the `_log_worker` is the \"真正消费日志队列\" location. Yes.\n\nLet me also check whether there's another consumer, e.g., a StatsLogger._emit... Just `_log_worker`.\n\nNow let me apply edits. I already have 